          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "How Much Does a Generator Cost",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-much-does-a-generator-cost/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much is a generator for a house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a portable that can run essentials or, with a 240V connection, most of a house, plan on about $600 to $1,600 for the generator and another $500 to $2,000 for a safe panel connection. An automatic standby generator typically costs $6,500 to $15,000 or more installed, depending on size, fuel and how far it sits from the panel and gas meter."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much is a portable generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Retail listings typically show 3,500 to 5,000 running watt open-frame portables at about $400 to $900, larger 7,500 to 10,000 watt units around $800 to $1,600, and inverter portables from about $400 for small value models to $1,800 or more for mid-size premium ones. Dual fuel versions usually add $50 to $200."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much is a small generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The smallest 1,000 to 1,200 watt gas inverters typically start around $250 to $400, and 2,000 to 2,500 watt inverters, the most useful small size for home essentials, usually run $400 to $700 from value brands and over $1,000 from premium brands. Avoid no-name units without CO shutoff or published running watts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it cheaper to buy or rent a generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For more than a handful of days a year, buying is usually cheaper. Rental shops commonly charge $50 to $150 a day for a mid-size portable, and rental stock tends to sell out before storms. Renting makes sense for a one-off planned need, such as a renovation, but not as an emergency plan."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does it cost to run a generator per day?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A mid-size gasoline open-frame at half load typically burns around half a gallon to a gallon an hour, so roughly $40 to $85 a day at $3.50 a gallon if it runs continuously. Inverters at light load use much less. Natural gas standby units are commonly estimated at a few dollars an hour, depending on size and local rates."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a dual fuel generator worth the extra cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, yes. The dual fuel version of a model typically costs $50 to $200 more than the gas-only one, and propane stores indefinitely, so you avoid stale gasoline and gummed carburettors. You get about 10 percent less power on propane, so size with that in mind. For most home backup buyers it is one of the best-value upgrades."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the cheapest way to back up a whole house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes, a 6,500 to 9,500 running watt portable with a 240V twist-lock, connected through an inlet box and an interlock kit installed by an electrician, is the lowest-cost way to power most circuits. It typically totals $1,800 to $3,500. You manage loads yourself and start the generator manually, which is the trade against a standby unit."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
