        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most home backup buyers spend $600 to $1,600 on the generator and another $500 to $2,000 connecting it safely to the house. Automatic standby units start around $6,500 installed. The cheapest watts are open-frame generators at roughly 10 to 20 cents per running watt; inverters cost two to four times that, and you should pay it only if you need quiet, clean power.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="price-by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Generator Prices by Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            The short answer to <strong>how much are generators</strong> is anywhere from about $300 to well over $15,000, and the type of generator decides which end of that range you land on far more than the brand does. The ranges below are what retail listings typically show in late 2026 for new units from established brands; sales, seasons and storm demand move them, sometimes by hundreds of dollars in a week.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Conventional Open-Frame Generators</h3>
          <p>
            Conventional open-frame generators are the cheapest watts you can buy. A 3,500 to 5,000 running watt open-frame typically sells for about $400 to $900, and larger 7,500 to 10,000 watt units usually land between about $800 and $1,600. They are loud and their power is less clean than an inverter's, but for a home backup that sits in the driveway they deliver the most power per dollar.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inverter Generators</h3>
          <p>
            Inverter generators cost more per watt. Small 2,000 to 2,500 watt inverters typically run from about $400 for value brands to $1,000 to $1,400 for premium Japanese brands. Mid-size 3,000 to 4,500 watt inverters usually sit between about $700 and $1,800, with premium models above that. You are paying for quieter running, cleaner power and better fuel economy at light load, which our <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generator</a> comparison weighs in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dual Fuel and Tri-Fuel Models</h3>
          <p>
            Dual fuel adds less than most people expect. On models sold in both versions, the dual fuel variant typically costs $50 to $200 more than the gasoline-only one, and tri-fuel units that add natural gas usually sit a little higher again. Given that propane stores indefinitely and gasoline does not, that premium is one of the better values in the category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Diesel Portables and Standby Units</h3>
          <p>
            At the top end, portable diesels and standby units are a different market. Generac's site lists its XD5000E diesel portable from about $4,019, roughly four to five times the price of a gasoline unit with similar output; our <a href="/best-diesel-generator/">diesel generator guide</a> explains who that is worth it for. Home standby generators are covered in depth in our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">Generac generator cost</a> breakdown, which puts typical installed prices at about $6,500 to $9,500 for 10 to 14 kW and $9,000 to $13,000 for 18 to 22 kW units.
          </p>

