        <div id="by-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Price by Size and Cost per Running Watt
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Cost per Running Watt Shows</h3>
          <p>
            The fairest way to compare generator prices is cost per running watt: the price divided by the running watts the manufacturer rates it for. It strips out the marketing habit of quoting starting or peak watts in large type and shows you what you are really paying for continuous power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Open-Frame Cost per Watt</h3>
          <p>
            Open-frame generators usually come out at roughly 10 to 20 cents per running watt. A 3,600 running watt unit at around $400 is about 11 cents; a 7,500 running watt unit at around $1,000 is about 13 cents. Large open-frames often get cheaper per watt as they get bigger, because the frame, wheels and controls cost about the same regardless of engine size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inverter Cost per Watt</h3>
          <p>
            Inverters typically sit at 25 to 60 cents per running watt. A 1,800 running watt inverter at around $600 is over 30 cents; a premium 1,800 watt model near $1,100 is around 60 cents. That gap is the price of quiet and clean power, and it is worth paying only if you need those things. Our <a href="/best-cheap-generator/">cheap generator guide</a> goes deeper on where the best value per watt sits at the budget end.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buying the Right Size</h3>
          <p>
            Running watts also tell you which size you need, and buying the wrong size is the most expensive mistake in the category. Too small and it will not start your well pump or furnace; too large and you pay for capacity you never use and burn extra fuel. Use our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> and <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">house sizing guide</a> before you price anything, and look at our breakdowns of <a href="/what-will-a-5000-watt-generator-run/">what a 5,000 watt generator will run</a> if you are near that common size.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Cost per Running Watt</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10-20¢</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Open-frame</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">25-60¢</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Inverter</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">80¢+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Diesel portable</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">40¢+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Standby, installed, per watt</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="small-portable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Is a Small or Portable Generator?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Smallest Inverters</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>How much is a small generator</strong> depends on what small means to you. Retail listings for the smallest gas inverters, around 1,000 to 1,200 watts, typically start near $250 to $400 from value brands. They run a laptop, phone chargers, a CPAP machine or a few lights, and not much else, and they make sense mostly for camping and tailgating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 2,000 to 2,500 Watt Class</h3>
          <p>
            The 2,000 to 2,500 watt class is where most buyers who want a small generator should look. It is the size that can keep a refrigerator, a freezer, a router and some lighting going during an outage, and value-brand inverters in that range typically sell for $400 to $700. Premium models with longer warranties and better dealer support usually cost $1,000 or more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Portable Generators for Home Backup</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>How much is a portable generator</strong> for home backup is a different question, because most homes need more than 2,500 watts to run a furnace blower, a sump pump and a fridge at the same time. A 4,000 to 5,000 running watt open-frame with electric start and a 240V outlet typically sells for $600 to $1,000, and that is the price point where most home backup purchases end up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Be Wary of the Cheapest Listings</h3>
          <p>
            Be wary of the cheapest listings in each class. Units priced far below the brand-name range often skip carbon monoxide shutoff, publish only peak watts, or come from sellers who will not be around for warranty claims. A few dollars saved there is rarely worth it; our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generators</a> list sticks to models with CO shutoff and published running watts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Hidden Costs: Connection, Cords and Fuel
          </h2>

          <p>
            The generator is rarely the whole bill. For home backup, plan for another $100 to $2,000 on top of the purchase price depending on how you connect it, and decide that before you choose a generator, because the connection method dictates which outlets the generator needs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Connecting to the house</h3>

          <p>
            The cheapest legal whole-panel connection is usually an interlock kit and a power inlet box. Inlet boxes typically cost $50 to $150, interlock kits for common panels typically $50 to $200, and an electrician's installation commonly adds a few hundred dollars to over $1,000 depending on panel location and local rates. A manual transfer switch costs more, often $300 to $700 for the hardware alone, plus installation. Our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch guide</a> compares the options, our guide to <a href="/how-to-hook-up-a-generator-to-your-house/">hooking a generator up to your house</a> walks through the process, and our <a href="/best-generator-inlet-box/">generator inlet box</a> picks cover the hardware.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Permits</h3>

          <p>
            Many jurisdictions require an electrical permit for an inlet and interlock or a transfer switch, and fees commonly run from about $50 to a few hundred dollars. Your electrician usually pulls it. Skipping the permit can complicate an insurance claim and a home sale, so it is cheap insurance in itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cords</h3>

          <p>
            A 30 amp, four-prong generator cord to feed an inlet box typically costs $70 to $200 depending on length and gauge, and a pair of heavy-duty extension cords for running appliances directly costs $40 to $100 more. Long runs need heavier wire; our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">extension cord sizing guide</a> covers the gauge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel and storage</h3>

          <p>
            Gas cans, stabiliser and oil for the first season usually add $50 to $120. Propane users need a tank or two: a filled 20 lb cylinder typically costs $50 to $70 new, then refills or exchanges. Most generators also need a cover or shelter if they will run in the rain, which is another $50 to $300.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
